  "summary": "A dry stone construction style used in the medieval city of Great Zimbabwe and similar sites across the region.",
  "description": "Characterized by massive stone walls without mortar, conical towers, and elliptical structures. Developed by the Shona people as political and religious centers, showcasing advanced engineering without the use of cement or mortar.",
  "culturalContext": "Great Zimbabwe (11th-15th c., UNESCO) — the medieval Shona capital — built Africa's largest ancient stone complex without mortar: the Great Enclosure's 11m-high walls of hand-fitted granite blocks, the conical tower, and hill-complex enclosures aligned to sacred astronomy. The name gave the modern nation its identity; colonial denial (crediting Phoenicians) was overturned by archaeological consensus, and the soapstone Zimbabwe Birds from the site are the national emblem.",
